  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Small-handed Leaf-cutter Bee (Megachile gemula) is a solitary bee species belonging to the family Megachilidae, known for its remarkable ability to precisely cut circular pieces from plant leaves. These native New England bees use the leaf fragments to construct individual brood cells within hollow stems or cavities.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Leaf-cutting behavior",
      "description": "Observe the distinctive circular cuts on leaves, which are characteristic of Megachile species and rarely found from other bee damage patterns",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Compact body size",
      "description": "Small-handed Leaf-cutter Bees are notably smaller than many other Megachile species, with a compact, robust body typically 8-10mm in length",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Abdominal pollen brush",
      "description": "Look for the distinctive scopa (pollen-collecting hairs) on the underside of the abdomen, appearing as a dense, brush-like structure",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Small-handed Leaf-cutter Bees thrive in open woodlands, gardens, and meadows throughout New England where flowering plants and suitable nesting cavities are available.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "These bees are incredibly precise architects—they can cut leaf fragments with mathematical accuracy and arrange them in their nesting tubes like a perfectly fitted mosaic, creating multiple individual cells for their offspring.",
